```js
function shellSort(array) {
  let gap = Math.floor(array.length / 2);
  while (gap > 0) {
    for (let i = gap; i < array.length; i += 1) {
      let temp = array[i];
      let j = i;
      while (j >= gap && array[j - gap] > temp) {
        array[j] = array[j - gap];
        j -= gap;
      }
      array[j] = temp;
    }
    gap = Math.floor(gap / 2);
  }
  return array; 
}
```

It works as follows:

1. It calculates an initial gap size as array.length / 2
2. While the gap is greater than 0:
3. It iterates through the array from the gap index to the end  
4. It takes the current element and a comparison index j
5. It shifts elements to the right as long as the element at j-gap is greater than the current element  
6. It assigns the current element to its sorted position
7. It halves the gap for the next iteration
8. It returns the sorted array

You can use it like this:

```js
const array = [5, 2, 4, 6, 1, 3];
const sortedArray = shellSort(array);
console.log(sortedArray); // [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
