[' Sure! Here's an example function that implements the shell sort algorithm to sort an array of integers:
```
function shellSort(arr) {
  let n = arr.length;
  let h = 1;

  while (h < n) {
    // compute the maximum gap
    let gap = Math.min(h, n - h);

    // sort the array using the gap
    for (let i = 0; i < n; i += gap) {
      let j = i + gap - 1;
      while (j >= i) {
        if (arr[j] <= arr[j - gap]) {
          break;
        }
        swap(arr, j, j - gap);
        j -= gap;
      }
    }

    // increase the gap
    h = 3 * h + 1;
  }
}

// helper function to swap two elements
function swap(arr, i, j) {
  let temp = arr[i];
  arr[i] = arr[j];
  arr[j] = temp;
}
```
The shell sort algorithm works by dividing the array into smaller subarrays, called "shells", and then sorting each shell individually. The size of the shells increases with each iteration of the algorithm.

In this implementation, we start by setting the length of the array `n` and the initial gap size `h` to 1. We then enter a loop that continues until the gap size exceeds the length of the array.

In each iteration of the loop, we first compute the maximum gap size `gap` that can be used to sort the array. We then use this gap size to sort the array by iterating over each element in the array, starting from the first element, and swapping it with the element `gap` positions ahead of it if the two elements are in the wrong order. We continue this process until we reach the end of the array or until the gap size is reduced to 0.

After sorting each shell, we increase the gap size by a factor of 3 and repeat the process until the entire array is sorted.

Note that the time complexity of this algorithm is O(n^2), which is slower than other sorting algorithms like quicksort and mergesort. However, the shell sort algorithm has a simpler implementation and can be useful for small arrays or for educational purposes.']

